= DOMAIN 1: NETWORK FUNDAMENTALS & OSI ARCHITECTURE (Q01 – Q20)

#interview-compact(num: 1, q: "Explain the life of a packet from a client browser to a web server across a routed network.", a: "1. Client resolves server domain to IP via DNS query (UDP 53). 2. Client initiates TCP 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK). 3. If destination IP is remote, client resolves default gateway MAC via ARP. 4. Frame travels to switch; switch forwards out trunk to default gateway router. 5. Router de-encapsulates Layer 2, inspects Layer 3 destination IP, performs Longest Prefix Match, decrements TTL, re-encapsulates in next-hop Layer 2 frame, and forwards.", follow_up: "What field changes at every Layer 3 hop? Answer: Source and Destination MAC addresses; IP addresses remain constant (unless NAT is used).")

#interview-compact(num: 2, q: "What is the difference between TCP Windowing and Flow Control?", a: "TCP Window Size (advertised in the TCP header) indicates how many bytes of data the receiver buffer can accept before requiring an acknowledgment. Flow control dynamically shrinks the window size (down to 0, pausing transmission) if the receiver CPU or application buffer becomes congested.", follow_up: "What is TCP Silly Window Syndrome? Answer: When receiver repeatedly advertises tiny 1-byte windows.")

#interview-compact(num: 3, q: "Explain the differences between Cat5e, Cat6, and Cat6a cabling in enterprise environments.", a: "Cat5e operates at 100 MHz, supporting 1 Gbps up to 100 meters. Cat6 operates at 250 MHz, supporting 10 Gbps up to 55 meters (or 1G at 100m). Cat6a operates at 500 MHz, supporting full 10 Gbps across the entire 100-meter channel link while eliminating Alien Crosstalk (ANEXT).", follow_up: "Why does Cat6 have a plastic spline? Answer: Separates wire pairs internally to prevent NEXT.")

#interview-compact(num: 4, q: "How do you subnet 10.0.0.0/8 to provide 500 subnets with at least 100 hosts each?", a: "To get at least 100 hosts: 2^H - 2 >= 100 -> H = 7 bits (2^7 - 2 = 126 hosts). Host bits required = 7. Subnet bits = 32 - 7 = 25. Mask = /25 (255.255.255.128). Subnets created from /8 to /25: 2^(25-8) = 2^17 = 131,072 subnets, vastly exceeding the 500 required.", follow_up: "What is the block size for /25? Answer: 128.")

#interview-compact(num: 5, q: "What is an IPv6 Solicited-Node Multicast address and how is it derived?", a: "It is an ICMPv6 multicast group used by Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP) to resolve MAC addresses without broadcasting. Prefix is ff02::1:ff00:0/104, formed by appending the last 24 bits (6 hex digits) of the target host's IPv6 address to ff02::1:ff.", follow_up: "Does NDP eliminate ARP? Answer: Yes, completely.")

#interview-compact(num: 6, q: "Compare TCP and UDP header overhead and explain when to select each.", a: "TCP header is 20-60 bytes (flags, sequence/ACK numbers, window size) providing reliable, ordered, byte-stream delivery with congestion control. UDP header is fixed at 8 bytes (Source/Dest Port, Length, Checksum) providing low-latency, connectionless best-effort delivery ideal for VoIP (RTP), DNS, DHCP, and SNMP.", follow_up: "Why does DNS use UDP for queries but TCP for zone transfers? Answer: Queries require rapid low-latency (<512 bytes); zone transfers exceed 512 bytes and require reliable delivery.")

#interview-compact(num: 7, q: "Define Protocol Data Units (PDUs) across all layers of the OSI model.", a: "Layer 7-5 (Application, Presentation, Session): Data / Payload. Layer 4 (Transport): Segment (TCP) or Datagram (UDP). Layer 3 (Network): Packet (encapsulates IP header). Layer 2 (Data Link): Frame (adds MAC header and FCS trailer). Layer 1 (Physical): Bits transmitted as electrical voltages, light pulses, or RF radio waves.", follow_up: "What is the minimum Ethernet frame size and why? Answer: 64 bytes (including 4-byte FCS); necessary for CSMA/CD collision detection slot time.")

#interview-compact(num: 8, q: "Explain the boundaries of Collision Domains vs Broadcast Domains.", a: "A Collision Domain is a physical network segment where data packets can collide with one another (Hubs create 1 shared domain; every switch port is an independent collision domain). A Broadcast Domain is the set of all devices that receive broadcast frames (Switches extend broadcast domains; Routers and VLANs break broadcast domains).", follow_up: "Does a router forward Layer 2 broadcast frames (FFFF.FFFF.FFFF)? Answer: No, routers drop Layer 2 broadcasts by default.")

#interview-compact(num: 9, q: "What is the difference between MTU and MSS, and what happens if DF bit is set?", a: "MTU (Maximum Transmission Unit) is the maximum Layer 3 packet size an interface can forward without fragmentation (default 1500 bytes for Ethernet). MSS (Maximum Segment Size) is the maximum TCP payload size (MTU minus 40 bytes of IP/TCP headers = 1460 bytes). If DF (Don't Fragment) bit is set and packet exceeds MTU, router drops it and returns ICMP Type 3 Code 4 (Fragmentation Needed).", follow_up: "What technology solves Path MTU black holes? Answer: PMTUD (Path MTU Discovery) and TCP MSS clamping ('ip tcp adjust-mss').")

#interview-compact(num: 10, q: "Specify RFC 1918 Private IPv4 address ranges and their enterprise use cases.", a: "Class A: 10.0.0.0/8 (10.0.0.0 to 10.255.255.255, 16.7M hosts) - large enterprise datacenters and campuses. Class B: 172.16.0.0/12 (172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255, 1M hosts across 16 contiguous /16 blocks). Class C: 192.168.0.0/16 (192.168.0.0 to 192.168.255.255, 256 /24 blocks) - small branch and SOHO offices. Not routable across the public Internet.", follow_up: "What is APIPA / Link-Local IPv4 range? Answer: 169.254.0.0/16, self-assigned when DHCP fails.")

#interview-compact(num: 11, q: "Explain ARP, Gratuitous ARP, and Proxy ARP mechanics.", a: "ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) resolves a known IPv4 address to an unknown MAC address via Layer 2 broadcast. Gratuitous ARP is an unprompted ARP reply broadcast by a host to announce its IP/MAC to the LAN, detecting duplicate IPs and updating switch CAM tables upon link failover (HSRP). Proxy ARP allows a router to answer ARP requests on behalf of a remote subnet.", follow_up: "Why should Proxy ARP usually be disabled? Answer: It hides misconfigurations and can be abused for Man-in-the-Middle attacks.")

#interview-compact(num: 12, q: "Detail the 4 major IPv6 address types and their standard prefix notations.", a: "1. Global Unicast (GUA): 2000::/3 (publicly routable). 2. Unique Local (ULA): fc00::/7 (fd00::/8 in practice, enterprise private). 3. Link-Local (LLA): fe80::/10 (fe80::/64 on local link, mandatory for routing neighbor adjacencies). 4. Multicast: ff00::/8 (replaces broadcast; ff02::1 all nodes, ff02::2 all routers). Loopback is ::1/128; Unspecified is ::/128.", follow_up: "Can an interface have multiple IPv6 addresses? Answer: Yes, an interface typically has at least one LLA and one GUA simultaneously.")

#interview-compact(num: 13, q: "Walk through the EUI-64 algorithm for generating a 64-bit interface ID from a MAC address.", a: "Given 48-bit MAC 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E: 1. Split MAC into two 24-bit halves: 00-1A-2B and 3C-4D-5E. 2. Insert FFFE in the middle: 00-1A-2B-FF-FE-3C-4D-5E. 3. Invert the 7th bit (Universal/Local bit) of the first byte: 00 (00000000) becomes 02 (00000010). Resulting Interface ID: 021A:2BFF:FE3C:4D5E.", follow_up: "Why is the 7th bit inverted? Answer: IEEE considers 0 as universally administered; inverting it makes locally assigned addresses 0 for human readability.")

#interview-compact(num: 14, q: "Compare SLAAC vs Stateful DHCPv6 vs Stateless DHCPv6.", a: "SLAAC (Stateless Address Autoconfiguration): Host learns /64 prefix from Router Advertisement (RA) via ICMPv6 NDP and generates host ID via EUI-64 or random (RFC 4941); no server needed. Stateless DHCPv6: Host uses SLAAC for IP address, but queries DHCPv6 server for DNS/domain name (O-flag=1). Stateful DHCPv6: Server manages pool and leases both IP address and options (M-flag=1).", follow_up: "What NDP messages are used in SLAAC? Answer: Router Solicitation (RS, ff02::2) and Router Advertisement (RA, ff02::1).")

#interview-compact(num: 15, q: "Compare Single-Mode Fiber (SMF) vs Multi-Mode Fiber (MMF).", a: "SMF has a narrow core (8-10 microns), uses laser light sources (1310nm / 1550nm), has zero modal dispersion, and supports long distances (10 km to 80 km) for campus backbones and WANs. MMF has a wide core (50 or 62.5 microns, OM1-OM5), uses cheap LED/VCSEL light (850nm / 1300nm), suffers from modal dispersion, and reaches up to 300m-500m inside datacenters.", follow_up: "What optical transceiver is standard for 10G MMF? Answer: 10GBASE-SR (850nm, reach up to 300m on OM3).")

#interview-compact(num: 16, q: "Explain Cisco 3-Tier Hierarchical vs 2-Tier Collapsed Core architectures.", a: "3-Tier consists of: Core Layer (high-speed packet switching, zero ACLs/QoS, redundant 100G links), Distribution Layer (policy enforcement, inter-VLAN routing, ACLs, aggregation), and Access Layer (end-user connectivity, PoE, port security). 2-Tier Collapsed Core merges Core and Distribution into a single redundant switch pair, reducing capital cost for small-to-medium enterprises.", follow_up: "What protocol provides default gateway redundancy at the distribution layer? Answer: FHRP (HSRP, VRRP).")

#interview-compact(num: 17, q: "Explain the Spine-Leaf datacenter topology and why it replaced 3-Tier in modern clouds.", a: "Spine-Leaf is a 2-stage clos architecture where every leaf switch connects to every spine switch, with zero connections between leaf switches and zero connections between spine switches. Provides predictable, deterministic latency (exactly 2 hops between any servers) and non-blocking East-West traffic flow using Layer 3 ECMP (Equal-Cost Multi-Path) instead of STP.", follow_up: "What protocol encapsulates Layer 2 across the Layer 3 spine-leaf fabric? Answer: VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN).")

#interview-compact(num: 18, q: "Detail Power over Ethernet (PoE) standards and power delivery budgets.", a: "802.3af (PoE): Delivers up to 15.4W at PSE (12.95W at PD), suitable for basic IP phones and IoT sensors. 802.3at (PoE+): Delivers up to 30W (25.5W at PD), supports dual-band APs and PTZ cameras. 802.3bt (4PPoE / PoE++): Type 3 delivers 60W (51W at PD); Type 4 delivers 90-100W across all 4 wire pairs, powering Wi-Fi 6/7 APs and smart building lighting.", follow_up: "What protocol negotiates fine-grained power requirements between switch and endpoint? Answer: LLDP-MED or CDP.")

#interview-compact(num: 19, q: "How does CSMA/CD operate in half-duplex Ethernet networks?", a: "Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection: Host listens to cable before transmitting (Carrier Sense). If idle, it transmits; multiple hosts can transmit simultaneously (Multiple Access). If collision occurs, colliding stations detect voltage spike, immediately abort transmission, emit a 32-bit Jam Signal to notify all stations, and calculate a random Backoff delay via truncated binary exponential backoff.", follow_up: "Does full-duplex Ethernet use CSMA/CD? Answer: No, full-duplex has dedicated Tx and Rx channels, eliminating collisions entirely.")

#interview-compact(num: 20, q: "Explain SFP, SFP+, and QSFP form factors and optical transceiver optics.", a: "SFP (Small Form-factor Pluggable) supports 1 Gbps. SFP+ supports 10 Gbps (uses same mechanical size as SFP). QSFP+ (Quad SFP) supports 40 Gbps (aggregating 4 x 10G lanes). QSFP28 supports 100 Gbps (4 x 25G lanes). Optics designations: SR (Short Range, MMF, 850nm, <=300m), LR (Long Range, SMF, 1310nm, <=10km), ER (Extended Range, SMF, 1550nm, <=40km).", follow_up: "Can you plug an SFP (1G) optic into an SFP+ (10G) switch port? Answer: Generally yes, port auto-negotiates down to 1G (check Cisco data sheet).")


