= DOMAIN 2: SWITCHING, VLANS & TRUNKING (Q21 – Q40)

#interview-compact(num: 21, q: "What is a Native VLAN mismatch and what security risks does it create?", a: "When connected switches on an 802.1Q trunk have different Native VLANs configured (e.g. SW1 native vlan 10, SW2 native vlan 20), untagged traffic on SW1 will be placed into VLAN 20 on SW2. This causes cross-VLAN traffic leakage and enables VLAN Hopping attacks.", follow_up: "How do you identify a native VLAN mismatch? Answer: 'CDP-4-NATIVE_VLAN_MISMATCH' console error.")

#interview-compact(num: 22, q: "Explain the difference between Dynamic Desirable and Dynamic Auto in Cisco DTP.", a: "Dynamic Trunking Protocol (DTP) negotiates trunks automatically. 'Dynamic Desirable' actively asks the neighbor to trunk; 'Dynamic Auto' listens passively and only trunks if the neighbor requests it. Auto-to-Auto results in an Access port; Desirable-to-Auto forms a Trunk.", follow_up: "Security best practice for DTP? Answer: Disable it via 'switchport nonegotiate'.")

#interview-compact(num: 23, q: "How does Rapid PVST+ (802.1w) achieve sub-second convergence without timers?", a: "RSTP uses an active Proposal/Agreement handshake over full-duplex point-to-point links. When a new link comes up, the designated switch sends a Proposal; the receiving switch blocks all non-edge ports (Sync) and returns an Agreement, instantly transitioning the link to Forwarding.", follow_up: "What are the port states in RSTP? Answer: Discarding, Learning, Forwarding.")

#interview-compact(num: 24, q: "What is the difference between LACP and PAgP in EtherChannel?", a: "LACP (Link Aggregation Control Protocol) is an open IEEE 802.3ad standard (modes: Active, Passive). PAgP (Port Aggregation Protocol) is a Cisco proprietary standard (modes: Desirable, Auto). Active bundles up to 8 links with 8 standby links.", follow_up: "Can Active bundle with Passive? Answer: Yes, forms channel.")

#interview-compact(num: 25, q: "What conditions must match across physical interfaces before forming an EtherChannel?", a: "1. Identical speed and duplex. 2. Identical switchport mode (all access or all trunk). 3. If access, identical VLAN ID. 4. If trunk, identical native VLAN and identical allowed VLAN list. 5. Identical STP cost.", follow_up: "What happens if one port speed differs? Answer: Port is placed in 'susp' (suspended) state.")

#interview-compact(num: 26, q: "Compare CAM Table (MAC address table) vs TCAM (Ternary CAM) in Cisco switches.", a: "CAM (Content Addressable Memory) stores Layer 2 MAC-to-port bindings with binary 0/1 matching, returning exact port in 1 clock cycle. TCAM (Ternary CAM) evaluates three states: 0, 1, and 'X' (don't care/wildcard), enabling wire-speed parallel evaluations of Layer 3 IP routing, ACLs, and QoS marking without CPU intervention.", follow_up: "What happens when the CAM table overflows during a MAC flooding attack? Answer: Switch defaults to hub behavior, flooding all unknown unicast frames out every port.")

#interview-compact(num: 27, q: "Explain the 4 core steps of Ethernet frame processing inside a switch.", a: "1. Learning: Switch inspects Source MAC; if absent from CAM, adds entry with ingress port and 300s aging timer. 2. Flooding: If Destination MAC is broadcast, multicast, or unknown unicast, floods out all ports in the VLAN except ingress. 3. Filtering: If Dest MAC is on the same port as source, drops frame. 4. Forwarding: If Dest MAC is known, forwards directly out recorded egress port.", follow_up: "What is the default aging time of a MAC address in Cisco IOS? Answer: 300 seconds (5 minutes).")

#interview-compact(num: 28, q: "Detail the internal structure of an IEEE 802.1Q tag header.", a: "802.1Q inserts a 4-byte (32-bit) tag between Source MAC and EtherType fields: 1. TPID (Tag Protocol Identifier, 16 bits): set to 0x8100. 2. TCI (Tag Control Information, 16 bits): Priority Code Point (PCP, 3 bits) for 802.1p QoS/CoS; Drop Eligible Indicator (DEI, 1 bit); VLAN ID (VID, 12 bits, supporting 4094 VLANs from 1 to 4094).", follow_up: "Why are VLANs 0 and 4095 reserved? Answer: VLAN 0 is for frame priority tagging; 4095 is reserved for system use.")

#interview-compact(num: 29, q: "Explain the Spanning Tree (802.1D) Root Bridge election algorithm.", a: "The switch with the lowest Bridge ID (BID) is elected Root Bridge. Bridge ID = Bridge Priority (2 bytes, default 32768 in multiples of 4096) + Extended System ID (VLAN ID) + Base MAC Address (6 bytes). Lowest Priority wins; if equal, lowest MAC address acts as tie-breaker.", follow_up: "How do you force a switch to become root for VLAN 10? Answer: 'spanning-tree vlan 10 root primary' or set priority to 4096.")

#interview-compact(num: 30, q: "Explain the interaction between STP PortFast and BPDU Guard.", a: "PortFast bypasses Listening and Learning states, immediately transitioning an access port to Forwarding (eliminating DHCP timeout delays on PCs). BPDU Guard listens on that port; if any BPDU is received (indicating a switch or rogue rogue device was plugged in), it immediately transitions the port into err-disabled state to prevent loops.", follow_up: "What command enables BPDU Guard globally on all PortFast ports? Answer: 'spanning-tree portfast bpduguard default'.")

#interview-compact(num: 31, q: "Compare STP Root Guard vs BPDU Filter.", a: "Root Guard is configured on distribution downstream ports to prevent connected switches from becoming Root Bridge; if a superior BPDU arrives, port transitions to 'root-inconsistent' state (blocking traffic). BPDU Filter disables BPDUs on a port; if enabled globally, sends a few BPDUs and stops; if enabled on interface, completely ignores incoming BPDUs (dangerous, can create loops).", follow_up: "Which is safer for enterprise access ports? Answer: Root Guard on designated downstream ports, BPDU Guard on access ports.")

#interview-compact(num: 32, q: "Compare Router-on-a-Stick (ROAS) vs Layer 3 Switch SVI Inter-VLAN Routing.", a: "ROAS uses a single physical trunk link to a router partitioned into subinterfaces (e.g. g0/0.10, g0/0.20) with 'encapsulation dot1Q'. Limited by physical link bandwidth (1 Gbps shared). Layer 3 switches use Switched Virtual Interfaces (SVIs, e.g. 'interface vlan 10') with high-speed internal switching bus routing packets at wire-speed in ASIC hardware.", follow_up: "What command enables Layer 3 routing on a Cisco multilayer switch? Answer: 'ip routing' in global configuration mode.")

#interview-compact(num: 33, q: "Explain Cisco Voice VLAN operation and CDP auxiliary VLAN tagging.", a: "A Cisco IP phone acts as a 3-port switch (connects to switch, PC, and internal phone DSP). Switchport is configured with 'switchport access vlan 10' (data) and 'switchport voice vlan 20'. Cisco switch uses CDP to instruct IP phone to tag its voice packets with 802.1Q VLAN 20 and CoS 5 (DSCP EF 46), while forwarding untagged PC data into VLAN 10.", follow_up: "What command verifies Voice VLAN status? Answer: 'show interfaces [id] switchport'.")

#interview-compact(num: 34, q: "What causes a Broadcast Storm and how does Storm Control mitigate it?", a: "A broadcast storm occurs when Layer 2 loops circulate broadcast or multicast frames indefinitely (since Ethernet frames lack a TTL field), saturating switch backplane and consuming 100% CPU. Storm Control monitors traffic levels over a 1-second interval; if broadcast traffic exceeds a configured threshold (e.g. 50%), it drops packets or puts port in err-disabled.", follow_up: "CLI command to enable storm control? Answer: 'storm-control broadcast level 50.0 30.0'.")

#interview-compact(num: 35, q: "Explain VTP (VLAN Trunking Protocol) modes, revision numbers, and the database corruption danger.", a: "VTP synchronizes VLAN databases across trunks. Modes: Server (creates/modifies VLANs, increments revision number), Client (learns VLANs, cannot edit), Transparent (forwards VTP advertisements, maintains local database). Danger: Connecting an old switch with a higher VTP revision number overwrites the production VLAN database across the entire enterprise, deleting production VLANs.", follow_up: "How do you safely reset VTP revision number to 0 before plugging in a switch? Answer: Change VTP domain name to a dummy name or change mode to Transparent.")

#interview-compact(num: 36, q: "Compare Cisco StackWise technology vs traditional uplink aggregation.", a: "StackWise physically interconnects up to 9 Catalyst switches via proprietary high-speed stack cables (up to 480 Gbps), combining them into a single logical switch with one shared management IP, single configuration file, and unified CAM/STP topology. Eliminates inter-switch STP loops and allows Cross-Stack EtherChannel.", follow_up: "If the stack master switch fails, what happens? Answer: An automatic election elects a new master based on priority, hardware feature set, and lowest MAC.")

#interview-compact(num: 37, q: "Explain EtherChannel load balancing hash algorithms and a common enterprise misconfiguration.", a: "EtherChannel hashes packet headers (src-mac, dst-mac, src-dst-ip, or src-dst-port) to select an egress physical link in the bundle. Misconfiguration: If load balancing is set to default 'src-mac' and all traffic originates from a single router MAC, all traffic traverses link 1 while other links sit completely idle.", follow_up: "What load balancing algorithm is recommended for routed enterprise traffic? Answer: 'port-channel load-balance src-dst-ip' or 'src-dst-port'.")

#interview-compact(num: 38, q: "Explain Unidirectional Link Detection (UDLD) and Loop Guard.", a: "UDLD monitors fiber optic links (where separate Tx and Rx strands exist) by exchanging Layer 2 keepalives. If Rx strand fails, link appears 'up' to physical layer but only transmits (unidirectional), causing STP loop. UDLD Normal mode marks port undetermined; Aggressive mode transitions port to err-disabled. Loop Guard prevents alternate/root ports from becoming designated when BPDUs stop arriving.", follow_up: "Where should Loop Guard be enabled? Answer: On STP root ports and alternate ports.")

#interview-compact(num: 39, q: "Compare Cisco Discovery Protocol (CDP) vs IEEE 802.1AB Link Layer Discovery Protocol (LLDP).", a: "CDP is Cisco proprietary (broadcasts every 60s, holdtime 180s) to discover neighbor device ID, capabilities, platform, and IP. LLDP is an open vendor-neutral standard (broadcasts every 30s, holdtime 120s) with Type-Length-Value (TLV) attributes. Security rule: Disable CDP/LLDP on untrusted, internet-facing, and user access ports to prevent network reconnaissance.", follow_up: "CLI commands to enable LLDP globally and on interface? Answer: 'lldp run' globally; 'lldp transmit' and 'lldp receive' on interface.")

#interview-compact(num: 40, q: "What exact forwarding decision does a switch make when receiving destination MAC FFFF.FFFF.FFFF?", a: "The switch identifies FFFF.FFFF.FFFF as the Layer 2 Broadcast address. It never updates CAM table with destination address, but floods the frame out of every active switchport belonging to the same VLAN as the ingress port, excluding the ingress port itself. It forwards the frame across all trunk links carrying that VLAN with 802.1Q tags.", follow_up: "What happens if no other ports belong to that VLAN? Answer: The frame is quietly dropped by the switch.")


