= DOMAIN 3: IP CONNECTIVITY & OSPF ROUTING (Q41 – Q60)

#interview-compact(num: 41, q: "Why does Longest Prefix Match (LPM) supersede Administrative Distance?", a: "Administrative Distance determines which route source is trusted when different protocols learn the exact same prefix. LPM determines which route in the active routing table is mathematically the most specific match for an incoming packet's destination IP.", follow_up: "If table has 10.1.0.0/16 (AD 90) and 10.1.1.0/24 (AD 110), packet for 10.1.1.1 chooses which? Answer: 10.1.1.0/24.")

#interview-compact(num: 42, q: "What are the 7 OSPF neighbor adjacency states in sequential order?", a: "1. Down: No Hello received. 2. Init: Hello received from neighbor, but local RID not listed. 3. 2-Way: Bi-directional communication confirmed (DR/BDR elected here). 4. ExStart: Master/Slave determined for DBD exchange. 5. Exchange: DBD packets describe LSDB. 6. Loading: LSR and LSU packets request and send missing LSAs. 7. Full: LSDBs are 100% identical.", follow_up: "In what state do DROthers stay with each other? Answer: 2-Way state.")

#interview-compact(num: 43, q: "Explain the difference between OSPF LSA Type 1, Type 2, and Type 3.", a: "Type 1 (Router LSA): Generated by every router, describes its own links and cost within its local area. Type 2 (Network LSA): Generated by the Designated Router (DR), lists all routers connected to the multi-access segment. Type 3 (Summary LSA): Generated by an Area Border Router (ABR), advertises inter-area networks into adjacent areas.", follow_up: "What generates Type 5 LSAs? Answer: Autonomous System Boundary Router (ASBR).")

#interview-compact(num: 44, q: "What causes two OSPF routers to fail to form a Full adjacency?", a: "1. Subnet mask mismatch. 2. Hello / Dead interval mismatch. 3. Area ID mismatch. 4. OSPF authentication password mismatch. 5. MTU mismatch (causes hang in ExStart). 6. Duplicate Router ID.", follow_up: "How do you bypass MTU check? Answer: 'ip ospf mtu-ignore'.")

#interview-compact(num: 45, q: "What is an OSPF Passive Interface and when is it configured?", a: "A passive interface prevents OSPF from transmitting Hello packets out that port, conserving CPU and bandwidth while preventing unauthorized neighbor relationships. Crucially, the interface subnet continues to be advertised into the OSPF domain. Applied on user LAN ports.", follow_up: "CLI command? Answer: 'passive-interface [interface]'.")

#interview-compact(num: 46, q: "Explain the Designated Router (DR) and Backup Designated Router (BDR) election in OSPF.", a: "On multi-access networks (Ethernet), DR/BDR prevents N*(N-1)/2 full mesh adjacencies, reducing to 2*N adjacencies. Routers form Full adjacency only with DR/BDR and stay in 2-Way with DROthers. Election: Highest OSPF Interface Priority (default 1, 0-255; 0 means never eligible). If tied, highest Router ID wins. Election is non-preemptive.", follow_up: "What multicast IP addresses do OSPF routers use? Answer: 224.0.0.5 (All OSPF Routers) and 224.0.0.6 (All DR/BDR Routers).")

#interview-compact(num: 47, q: "List the Administrative Distance (AD) hierarchy of common Cisco routing sources.", a: "Connected: 0; Static Route: 1; eBGP: 20; EIGRP (Internal): 90; OSPF: 110; IS-IS: 115; RIP: 120; EIGRP (External): 170; iBGP: 200; DHCP Default Route: 254; Unusable/Unknown: 255.", follow_up: "Can Administrative Distance be modified? Answer: Yes, by specifying custom distance at end of 'ip route' or in routing protocol configuration.")

#interview-compact(num: 48, q: "Explain Floating Static Routes and their configuration for automatic failover.", a: "A floating static route is an alternate static route configured with an Administrative Distance higher than the primary routing source (e.g. primary OSPF route has AD 110; backup static route has AD 120). The backup route remains dormant and hidden from the routing table until the primary link fails, at which point it automatically becomes active.", follow_up: "CLI configuration syntax? Answer: 'ip route 192.168.20.0 255.255.255.0 10.2.2.2 120'.")

#interview-compact(num: 49, q: "Compare Default Route (0.0.0.0/0) vs Gateway of Last Resort.", a: "A default route (0.0.0.0/0 with mask 0.0.0.0) matches every packet that has no specific match in the routing table. When installed in the routing table, it becomes the 'Gateway of Last Resort'. If a router receives a packet for an unknown destination and has no default route, it drops the packet and emits ICMP Type 3 Destination Unreachable.", follow_up: "CLI command on Cisco IOS? Answer: 'ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 [next-hop-ip | exit-interface]'.")

#interview-compact(num: 50, q: "Explain OSPF Reference Bandwidth and the formula used to calculate link cost.", a: "OSPF cost = Reference Bandwidth / Interface Bandwidth (in bps). By default, Reference Bandwidth is 10^8 bps (100 Mbps). Consequently, 100 Mbps, 1 Gbps, 10 Gbps, and 100 Gbps all receive cost 1, preventing OSPF from distinguishing faster links. Resolution: Adjust reference bandwidth to 100,000 (100 Gbps) using 'auto-cost reference-bandwidth 100000'.", follow_up: "Must the reference bandwidth match on all OSPF routers? Answer: Yes, otherwise cost calculations will be asymmetrical, creating sub-optimal routing.")

#interview-compact(num: 51, q: "Explain the architecture of Multi-Area OSPF and the role of Area 0.", a: "Multi-Area OSPF segments large networks to localize SPF calculations and contain link flaps. Area 0 (Backbone Area) is mandatory; all non-backbone areas (Area 1, 2, etc.) must physically attach to Area 0. Routers on boundary are Area Border Routers (ABRs), which generate Type 3 Summary LSAs and perform route summarization.", follow_up: "What mechanism connects a partitioned area back to Area 0 across another area? Answer: OSPF Virtual-Link.")

#interview-compact(num: 52, q: "Explain the risk of configuring a static route pointing only to an exit broadcast interface.", a: "When 'ip route 192.168.2.0 255.255.255.0 GigabitEthernet0/0' is configured on an Ethernet interface, the router assumes every host in 192.168.2.0/24 is directly connected to that wire. It broadcasts an ARP request for every single destination IP, overwhelming the router ARP cache and exhausting switch memory. Always configure the Next-Hop IP on multi-access links.", follow_up: "When is specifying only an exit interface safe? Answer: Only on Point-to-Point links (HDLC, PPP, GRE).")

#interview-compact(num: 53, q: "Compare Link-State (OSPF) vs Distance-Vector (RIP) vs Path-Vector (BGP).", a: "Distance-Vector (RIP): 'Routing by rumor'; routers periodically share full routing tables with direct neighbors only (Bellman-Ford algorithm). Link-State (OSPF): Every router flooded LSAs builds an identical topological map of the entire area, then independently computes the shortest path tree using Dijkstra's algorithm. Path-Vector (BGP): Exchanges BGP Autonomous System (AS-Path) lists to enforce inter-domain routing policies and prevent loops.", follow_up: "Which protocol converges fastest? Answer: Link-State protocols (OSPF, IS-IS) converge in milliseconds.")

#interview-compact(num: 54, q: "What is Equal-Cost Multi-Path (ECMP) routing and how does Cisco IOS handle it?", a: "ECMP occurs when a router learns multiple paths to the exact same destination prefix with identical metric and identical administrative distance. Cisco IOS installs up to 4 equal-cost paths by default (configurable up to 16/32/64) and load balances traffic across links per flow (based on IP 5-tuple hash).", follow_up: "Does OSPF support unequal-cost load balancing? Answer: No, only EIGRP supports unequal-cost load balancing (via the 'variance' command).")

#interview-compact(num: 55, q: "Explain the OSPF Router ID (RID) election criteria in Cisco IOS.", a: "OSPF selects its 32-bit Router ID using the following strict hierarchy: 1. Manually configured 'router-id [x.x.x.x]' under OSPF process. 2. Highest IPv4 address on any active Loopback interface. 3. Highest IPv4 address on any active physical interface in 'up/up' state. Once elected, the RID does not change dynamically unless the OSPF process is restarted.", follow_up: "Command to apply a new Router ID without rebooting router? Answer: 'clear ip ospf process'.")

#interview-compact(num: 56, q: "Compare Point-to-Point vs Broadcast OSPF network types.", a: "Point-to-Point (e.g. GRE tunnel, serial): Connects exactly 2 routers; DR/BDR election is completely bypassed; Hello timer is 10s, Dead timer is 40s; uses multicast 224.0.0.5. Broadcast (e.g. Ethernet): Connects multiple routers; elects DR/BDR; Hello timer is 10s, Dead timer is 40s; uses both 224.0.0.5 and 224.0.0.6.", follow_up: "What command overrides the default OSPF network type on an interface? Answer: 'ip ospf network point-to-point'.")

#interview-compact(num: 57, q: "Explain how OSPFv2 cryptographic authentication is configured in modern Cisco IOS.", a: "OSPF supports plaintext (insecure) and MD5/SHA authentication. Configuration: On interface, enable authentication: 'ip ospf authentication message-digest'. Define key ID and secret: 'ip ospf message-digest-key 1 md5 CiscoPass2027!'. Both Key ID and password must match exactly across the link, or neighbor adjacency drops.", follow_up: "What show command verifies OSPF authentication? Answer: 'show ip ospf interface [id]'.")

#interview-compact(num: 58, q: "Explain Route Summarization in OSPF and identify where it must be configured.", a: "Route summarization consolidates multiple contiguous subnets into a single advertisement, shrinking routing tables and preventing route flaps from triggering SPF recalculations across areas. In OSPF, inter-area summarization can ONLY be configured on ABRs ('area [id] range [prefix] [mask]'), and external route summarization can ONLY be configured on ASBRs ('summary-address [prefix] [mask]').", follow_up: "Can internal routers within an area summarize their own routes? Answer: No, all routers within an OSPF area must maintain an identical link-state database.")

#interview-compact(num: 59, q: "Detail 4 classic routing loop prevention mechanisms used in distance-vector protocols.", a: "1. Split Horizon: Never advertise a route back out the same interface from which it was learned. 2. Route Poisoning: When a route fails, immediately advertise its metric as infinity (16 in RIP). 3. Poison Reverse: Overrides split horizon by acknowledging the poisoned route with metric infinity. 4. Holddown Timers: Suppresses new routing information for a period (180s) to allow network stabilization.", follow_up: "Does OSPF need split horizon? Answer: No, OSPF uses Dijkstra's algorithm and area hierarchy to prevent loops.")

#interview-compact(num: 60, q: "Compare OSPFv2 (IPv4) vs OSPFv3 (IPv6) architectural differences.", a: "OSPFv3 is updated for IPv6 (RFC 5340): 1. Retains 32-bit dotted-decimal Router IDs (must be configured manually if no IPv4 addresses exist). 2. Runs per-link rather than per-subnet. 3. Uses Link-Local IPv6 addresses (fe80::/10) as next-hops and for neighbor adjacencies. 4. Multicast addresses: ff02::5 (All OSPF) and ff02::6 (All DR/BDR). 5. Replaces Type 1/2 IP addressing roles with new Intra-Area Prefix LSAs (Type 9).", follow_up: "Can OSPFv3 route both IPv4 and IPv6 simultaneously? Answer: Yes, using Address Families ('ospfv3 ipv4 address-family').")


