= DOMAIN 4: IP SERVICES (DHCP, NAT & NTP) (Q61 – Q80)

#interview-compact(num: 61, q: "Explain how a DHCP Relay Agent (ip helper-address) works at the packet level.", a: "When a client broadcasts a DHCPDISCOVER (src: 0.0.0.0, dst: 255.255.255.255, UDP 67), the router interface configured with 'ip helper-address [server-ip]' intercepts the broadcast, inserts its own interface IP into the 'giaddr' (Gateway IP Address) field, converts the packet to unicast, and routes it to the DHCP server.", follow_up: "Why is giaddr critical? Answer: It tells the DHCP server which IP subnet pool to draw from!")

#interview-compact(num: 62, q: "What is the difference between Static NAT, Dynamic NAT, and PAT?", a: "Static NAT: 1-to-1 permanent mapping between a private IP and public IP (used for inbound web/mail servers). Dynamic NAT: 1-to-1 mapping from a pool of public IPs on a first-come, first-served basis. PAT (Port Address Translation / NAT Overload): Many-to-1 mapping multiplexed via unique source port numbers.", follow_up: "What port range does PAT use? Answer: Ephemeral ports 1024 to 65535.")

#interview-compact(num: 63, q: "Explain the purpose of Network Time Protocol (NTP) in enterprise logging and security.", a: "NTP synchronizes device clocks across the enterprise (Stratum hierarchy). Accurate time synchronization is critical for correlating security incidents across firewalls, authentication servers, and syslog servers, and for validating X.509 digital certificate expiration dates.", follow_up: "What port does NTP use? Answer: UDP port 123.")

#interview-compact(num: 64, q: "What is SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol) and what are its versions?", a: "SNMP monitors network device health (CPU, bandwidth, errors) using Managers and Agents with MIBs (Management Information Bases). v1/v2c transmit community strings in clear plaintext (security risk). v3 provides message integrity, authentication (SHA), and encryption (AES-256).", follow_up: "What is an SNMP Trap? Answer: Unsolicited alert sent by agent to manager on UDP 162.")

#interview-compact(num: 65, q: "What is Syslog and what are its 8 severity levels?", a: "Syslog (UDP 514) logs system event messages. Severity levels 0 to 7: 0: Emergency, 1: Alert, 2: Critical, 3: Error, 4: Warning, 5: Notice, 6: Informational, 7: Debugging (mnemonic: 'Every Adult Cat Eats Warm Noodles In Darkness').", follow_up: "Which level generates the most CPU overhead? Answer: Level 7 (Debugging).")

#interview-compact(num: 66, q: "Compare First Hop Redundancy Protocols (FHRP): HSRP, VRRP, and GLBP.", a: "HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol, RFC 2281): Cisco proprietary, active/standby model, virtual MAC 0000.0c07.acXX (v1) or 0000.0c9f.fXXX (v2). VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol, RFC 5798): Open IETF standard, master/backup model, virtual MAC 0000.5e00.01XX. GLBP (Gateway Load Balancing Protocol): Cisco proprietary, provides active/active gateway load balancing using 1 AVG and up to 4 AVFs.", follow_up: "What happens in HSRP if preemption is NOT enabled? Answer: A recovered primary router with higher priority will NOT reclaim the active role from the backup.")

#interview-compact(num: 67, q: "Explain HSRP Preemption and why preemption delay is recommended in production.", a: "Preemption allows a router with higher priority to immediately assume the Active gateway role when it boots up. However, in production, if a router preempts before its interior routing protocol (OSPF/BGP) has fully converged, it drops transit traffic. 'standby [group] preempt delay minimum 60' forces it to wait 60 seconds before taking over.", follow_up: "Default HSRP priority? Answer: 100 (range 1-255).")

#interview-compact(num: 68, q: "Detail the 4-step DHCP DORA process and client renewal timers.", a: "1. Discover: Client broadcasts DHCPDISCOVER (UDP 67). 2. Offer: Server unicasts/broadcasts DHCPOFFER (UDP 68) with offered IP. 3. Request: Client broadcasts DHCPREQUEST accepting the offer. 4. Acknowledge: Server sends DHCPACK finalizing lease. Timers: T1 timer (at 50% of lease) client sends unicast Request to renew; T2 timer (at 87.5% of lease) client re-broadcasts Request to any available server.", follow_up: "What happens if lease reaches 100% without renewal? Answer: Host releases IP and falls back to APIPA (169.254.x.x) or re-initiates DORA.")

#interview-compact(num: 69, q: "Explain the hierarchical DNS resolution process from browser to authoritative server.", a: "1. Client checks local DNS cache / hosts file. 2. Client queries Local Recursive DNS Resolver (ISP/Corporate). 3. Resolver queries Root Name Server ('.' - 13 root clusters worldwide). 4. Root directs resolver to Top-Level Domain (TLD) server (.com, .org). 5. TLD server directs resolver to Authoritative Name Server for domain. 6. Authoritative server returns A/AAAA record; resolver caches response and answers client.", follow_up: "What is the difference between Recursive and Iterative DNS queries? Answer: Recursive queries demand a definitive answer; Iterative queries return referrals to the next server.")

#interview-compact(num: 70, q: "Explain Inside Local, Inside Global, Outside Local, and Outside Global NAT terms.", a: "Inside Local: The private IPv4 address assigned to a host on the inside enterprise LAN (e.g. 192.168.1.50). Inside Global: The registered public IPv4 address that represents the inside host to the outside world (e.g. 203.0.113.5). Outside Local: The IP address of an outside host as known to inside network. Outside Global: The actual public IP assigned to an outside host on the public Internet.", follow_up: "In standard PAT overload, which address is shared by all inside clients? Answer: Inside Global address.")

#interview-compact(num: 71, q: "How do IP SLA and Object Tracking enhance static route reliability?", a: "Standard static routes only drop from the routing table if the local physical interface goes down. If a remote ISP gateway freezes while interface stays up, static route remains active (black hole). IP SLA actively pings the remote gateway (e.g. every 5s); an Object Track monitors the SLA. If pings fail, the track goes down, immediately removing the static route and activating a backup floating route.", follow_up: "CLI syntax to link static route to track? Answer: 'ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 203.0.113.1 track 1'.")

#interview-compact(num: 72, q: "Explain NetFlow architecture and the 7 key fields that define a flow.", a: "NetFlow captures telemetry on IP traffic crossing interfaces. A unique flow is defined by matching 7 packet attributes: 1. Source IP, 2. Destination IP, 3. Source Port, 4. Destination Port, 5. Layer 3 Protocol (TCP/UDP/ICMP), 6. Ingress Interface, 7. Type of Service (ToS / DSCP byte). Packets sharing all 7 keys increment flow byte and packet counters.", follow_up: "What tool receives and graphs NetFlow packets? Answer: NetFlow Collector (e.g. SolarWinds, PRTG, Cisco Stealthwatch).")

#interview-compact(num: 73, q: "Detail Quality of Service (QoS) pillars: Classification, Marking, Queuing, and Shaping/Policing.", a: "Classification: Identifies traffic types (VoIP, video, web). Marking: Colors packet headers with priority (Layer 2 CoS 3-bit in 802.1Q; Layer 3 DSCP 6-bit in IP ToS byte; VoIP = DSCP EF 46). Queuing: Schedules packets into buffers (LLQ - Low Latency Queuing gives strict priority to voice). Policing: Drops or remarks traffic exceeding rate limit (hard cutoff). Shaping: Buffers excess packets to smooth traffic bursts (reduces packet drops).", follow_up: "What is the maximum acceptable one-way latency for toll-quality VoIP? Answer: 150 ms (jitter < 30 ms, loss < 1%).")

#interview-compact(num: 74, q: "Compare TFTP vs FTP vs SFTP in Cisco network operations.", a: "TFTP (Trivial FTP): Uses UDP port 69, has zero authentication or encryption, minimal memory footprint; used by Cisco routers to download IOS bin files during ROMMON recovery and backup configs. FTP: Uses TCP 20/21, requires username/password but transmits in plaintext. SFTP: Uses SSH TCP port 22, provides strong AES encryption and authentication; standard for automated script backups.", follow_up: "Can TFTP transfer files larger than 4GB? Answer: Classic TFTP (RFC 1350) was limited to 32MB; modern blocksize options support up to 4GB.")

#interview-compact(num: 75, q: "List high-frequency ICMP Types and Codes used in network troubleshooting.", a: "ICMP Type 8 Code 0: Echo Request (Ping transmit). ICMP Type 0 Code 0: Echo Reply (Ping receive). ICMP Type 3: Destination Unreachable (Code 0: Net Unreachable, Code 1: Host Unreachable, Code 3: Port Unreachable, Code 4: Fragmentation Needed and DF set). ICMP Type 11 Code 0: Time Exceeded (TTL expired in transit; used by Traceroute).", follow_up: "Which ICMP type indicates a firewall blocked the port? Answer: Type 3 Code 13 (Communication Administratively Prohibited).")

#interview-compact(num: 76, q: "Explain how Traceroute operates under the hood using IP TTL and ICMP.", a: "Traceroute discovers Layer 3 hops by systematically sending packets with incrementing TTL values: 1. Sends packet with TTL=1; first router decrements TTL to 0, drops packet, and sends back ICMP Type 11 Time Exceeded. Traceroute logs router IP and round-trip time. 2. Sends packet with TTL=2; second router expires it and replies. 3. Repeats until packet reaches destination, which replies with ICMP Echo Reply (Windows ping) or ICMP Port Unreachable (Linux UDP).", follow_up: "Why do asterisks (***) appear in traceroute output? Answer: Router or firewall along the path drops ICMP or rate-limits Time Exceeded generation.")

#interview-compact(num: 77, q: "What happens if two rogue DHCP servers are connected to the same Layer 2 VLAN?", a: "DHCP is a race condition. When a client broadcasts DHCPDISCOVER, both servers send DHCPOFFER. The client accepts whichever Offer arrives first (usually the physically closer server) and broadcasts DHCPREQUEST. If a rogue DHCP server responds faster with incorrect IP/DNS/Gateway, it hijacks client traffic (Man-in-the-Middle) or causes IP conflicts.", follow_up: "How do you stop this on a Cisco switch? Answer: Enable DHCP Snooping and mark only uplink ports as trusted.")

#interview-compact(num: 78, q: "What is NAT Hairpinning (NAT Loopback) and why is it necessary for internal servers?", a: "NAT Hairpinning occurs when an internal client attempts to access an internal server using the server's public Inside Global IP address. Without hairpinning, the router translates destination IP but leaves source IP unchanged; server replies directly to client private IP, but client drops reply because it expected a response from the public IP. Hairpinning translates both source and destination.", follow_up: "Alternative solution without hairpinning? Answer: Split-Horizon DNS (internal DNS returns private IP, external returns public IP).")

#interview-compact(num: 79, q: "What diagnostic information is exposed by 'show cdp neighbors detail' vs 'show ip protocols'?", a: "'show cdp neighbors detail' exposes connected neighbor device ID, local/remote port, IP address, exact Cisco IOS software release string, and hardware platform. 'show ip protocols' dumps active routing protocol parameters: protocol name (OSPF/EIGRP), router ID, active administrative distance, metric weights, network statements, passive interfaces, and routing information sources.", follow_up: "Which command reveals if OSPF passive-interface is active? Answer: 'show ip protocols' under 'Passive Interface(s)' section.")

#interview-compact(num: 80, q: "In Wireshark packet analysis, what causes TCP Retransmissions and Duplicate ACKs?", a: "TCP Retransmission occurs when a sender does not receive an ACK before its Retransmission Timeout (RTO) expires, indicating packet loss, severe congestion, or physical link errors. Duplicate ACKs occur when receiver receives out-of-order segments (e.g. segments 1, 3, 4 arrived, but 2 was lost); receiver repeats ACK for byte 1. 3 Duplicate ACKs trigger TCP Fast Retransmit without waiting for RTO.", follow_up: "What does a TCP RST (Reset) flag signify? Answer: Immediate connection teardown; sent when port is closed or firewall rejects session.")


