= DOMAIN 5: NETWORK SECURITY & ACCESS CONTROL (Q81 – Q100)

#interview-compact(num: 81, q: "What is the difference between a Standard ACL and an Extended ACL in Cisco IOS?", a: "Standard ACLs (1–99, 1300–1999) evaluate ONLY Source IP address; place close to the destination. Extended ACLs (100–199, 2000–2699) evaluate Protocol (TCP/UDP/ICMP), Source IP/Port, and Destination IP/Port; place close to the source.", follow_up: "How do you calculate wildcard masks? Answer: Invert subnet mask (255.255.255.255 - Mask).")

#interview-compact(num: 82, q: "What is DHCP Snooping and how does it prevent rogue DHCP server attacks?", a: "DHCP Snooping is a Layer 2 security feature on switches that classifies ports as Trusted (uplinks to legitimate DHCP servers) or Untrusted (user access ports). The switch drops DHCP Offer and Ack packets arriving on untrusted ports, preventing rogue servers from hijacking default gateways.", follow_up: "What table does DHCP Snooping build? Answer: DHCP Snooping Binding Database.")

#interview-compact(num: 83, q: "What is Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI) and how does it prevent ARP Poisoning?", a: "DAI intercepts all ARP requests and replies on untrusted ports and validates the IP-to-MAC binding against the trusted DHCP Snooping binding table. If an attacker attempts to claim another host's IP with a spoofed MAC (gratuitous ARP), DAI drops the frame and emits an alarm.", follow_up: "What prerequisite does DAI require? Answer: DHCP Snooping must be enabled first.")

#interview-compact(num: 84, q: "Explain the three AAA framework components (Authentication, Authorization, Accounting).", a: "Authentication: Verifies who the user is (username/password, MFA). Authorization: Determines what resources and commands the user is allowed to access (privilege levels, command authorization). Accounting: Records what the user actually did, tracking commands executed and session durations via TACACS+ or RADIUS.", follow_up: "Which protocol separates Authentication and Authorization? Answer: TACACS+ (TCP 49).")

#interview-compact(num: 85, q: "What is the difference between TACACS+ and RADIUS?", a: "TACACS+ is Cisco proprietary, uses TCP port 49, separates AAA into distinct modular functions, and encrypts the ENTIRE packet payload. RADIUS is an open IETF standard (UDP 1812/1813), combines Authentication and Authorization, and encrypts only the password field.", follow_up: "Which is preferred for device administration? Answer: TACACS+.")

#interview-compact(num: 86, q: "Explain the three Switchport Port Security violation modes: Protect, Restrict, and Shutdown.", a: "Protect: Drops frames from unauthorized MACs; does NOT log violations; port remains up. Restrict: Drops frames from unauthorized MACs; increments violation counter; sends Syslog message and SNMP trap; port remains up. Shutdown: Drops frames; immediately transitions interface into err-disabled state; turns physical LED solid amber; logs Syslog message.", follow_up: "Default violation mode in Cisco IOS? Answer: Shutdown mode.")

#interview-compact(num: 87, q: "How do you recover an err-disabled interface manually and automatically?", a: "Manual: 1. Disconnect offending device. 2. Enter interface mode, execute 'shutdown', then 'no shutdown'. Automatic: Configure err-disable timeout recovery globally using 'errdisable recovery cause [cause]' (e.g. psecure-violation) and set recovery interval using 'errdisable recovery interval [seconds]' (e.g. 300s).", follow_up: "Command to see why an interface is err-disabled? Answer: 'show errdisable recovery' or 'show interfaces status err-disabled'.")

#interview-compact(num: 88, q: "Compare Cisco password encryption types: Type 0, Type 7, Type 5, Type 8, and Type 9.", a: "Type 0: Clear plaintext (unencrypted). Type 7: Weak reversible Vigenere cipher generated by 'service password-encryption' (instantly reversible). Type 5: Irreversible MD5 hash ('enable secret'). Type 8: PBKDF2 with SHA-256 hash (stronger against dictionary attacks). Type 9: Scrypt hash (highly resistant to hardware ASIC brute-force).", follow_up: "Command to set a Type 9 password? Answer: 'enable algorithm-type scrypt secret [password]'.")

#interview-compact(num: 89, q: "Detail the exact steps to harden and enforce SSH v2 on a Cisco router.", a: "1. Configure hostname: 'hostname R1'. 2. Configure IP domain name: 'ip domain-name enterprise.local'. 3. Generate 2048-bit RSA key: 'crypto key generate rsa modulus 2048'. 4. Enforce SSH v2: 'ip ssh version 2'. 5. Create admin credentials: 'username admin privilege 15 secret StrongPass2027!'. 6. Lock down VTY: 'line vty 0 4' -> 'transport input ssh' -> 'login local' -> 'exec-timeout 5 0'.", follow_up: "Why specify modulus 2048 instead of 1024? Answer: Modulus < 2048 is cryptographically deprecated and vulnerable to factorization.")

#interview-compact(num: 90, q: "How do you secure VTY lines using an Access Control List (ACL)?", a: "Create a standard ACL permitting only management workstations or jump servers: 'access-list 10 permit 192.168.1.100'. Apply inbound to virtual terminal lines: 'line vty 0 4' followed by 'access-class 10 in'. Any IP not listed in ACL 10 will be rejected at the TCP handshake level, preventing brute-force SSH attacks.", follow_up: "What command verifies which IP addresses are currently connected to VTY lines? Answer: 'show users' or 'show ssh'.")

#interview-compact(num: 91, q: "Compare WPA2-Personal, WPA2-Enterprise, and WPA3 wireless security.", a: "WPA2-Personal (PSK): Uses a single shared Pre-Shared Key; vulnerable to offline 4-way handshake dictionary attacks. WPA2-Enterprise: Uses IEEE 802.1X with individual user credentials authenticated via RADIUS server (Cisco ISE) and dynamic AES-CCMP keys per user. WPA3: Implements Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE) dragon-fly handshake, immune to offline dictionary attacks, with 192-bit cryptographic suite.", follow_up: "What is Protected Management Frames (PMF)? Answer: 802.11w standard that encrypts de-authentication and disassociation frames to prevent Wi-Fi jamming.")

#interview-compact(num: 92, q: "Explain the three entities in the IEEE 802.1X Port-Based Authentication model.", a: "1. Supplicant: Software client running on user endpoint (PC, phone) requesting access. 2. Authenticator: Layer 2 switch or Wireless LAN Controller (WLC) controlling physical access; keeps port in unauthorized state until authenticated. 3. Authentication Server: Centralized RADIUS/TACACS+ server (e.g. Cisco ISE) storing credentials and authorization policies (EAP-TLS, PEAP).", follow_up: "What protocol carries authentication between Supplicant and Authenticator? Answer: EAPoL (EAP over LAN).")

#interview-compact(num: 93, q: "Detail 4 major Layer 2 attacks and their Cisco mitigation features.", a: "1. MAC Flooding: Fills CAM table -> Mitigated by Switchport Port Security. 2. Rogue DHCP: Unofficial DHCP server -> Mitigated by DHCP Snooping. 3. ARP Poisoning / Spoofing: Malicious Gratuitous ARP -> Mitigated by Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI). 4. VLAN Hopping (Double-Tagging): Exploit native VLAN -> Mitigated by assigning unused dummy Native VLAN (e.g. 999) and disabling DTP ('switchport nonegotiate').", follow_up: "Can an attacker perform double-tagging if native VLAN is tagged? Answer: No, double-tagging relies on native VLAN being untagged.")

#interview-compact(num: 94, q: "Compare Traditional Stateful Firewalls vs Next-Generation Firewalls (NGFW).", a: "Traditional Stateful Firewall (Cisco ASA): Inspects Layer 3/4 headers (IP, ports), maintains connection state table; unable to see inside encrypted payloads or detect evasive application ports. Next-Generation Firewall (Cisco Secure Firewall / Firepower): Operates at Layer 7 with Deep Packet Inspection (DPI), Application Visibility & Control (AVC), integrated Snort IPS, malware sandbox analysis, and SSL/TLS decryption.", follow_up: "How does an NGFW identify an app using non-standard ports (e.g. SSH over port 80)? Answer: Inspects Layer 7 application packet signatures, ignoring the destination port number.")

#interview-compact(num: 95, q: "Explain IPsec Site-to-Site VPN: IKE Phase 1 vs IKE Phase 2.", a: "IKE Phase 1 (ISAKMP SA): Authenticates peers (Pre-Shared Key or RSA certificates) and establishes a secure, encrypted management tunnel using Diffie-Hellman (DH Group 14/19) key exchange. IKE Phase 2 (IPsec SA): Negotiates parameters to protect actual user traffic; establishes unidirectional Security Associations (SAs) using ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload) with AES encryption and SHA hashing.", follow_up: "What protocol number does IPsec ESP use? Answer: IP Protocol 50 (AH is Protocol 51).")

#interview-compact(num: 96, q: "Compare Symmetric vs Asymmetric Encryption algorithms and performance.", a: "Symmetric: Uses identical shared secret key for encryption and decryption (AES-128, AES-256, ChaCha20); extremely fast in hardware ASICs, ideal for bulk data transport. Asymmetric: Uses a mathematically linked keypair—Public Key encrypts, Private Key decrypts (RSA-2048, ECC Curve25519); computationally expensive, used for digital signatures and initial session key exchange.", follow_up: "Which is used during HTTPS TLS handshake? Answer: Asymmetric for server authentication and key exchange; Symmetric (AES) for subsequent web data.")

#interview-compact(num: 97, q: "Detail the three classical factors of Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).", a: "1. Knowledge Factor (Something you know): Passwords, PINs, passphrases. 2. Possession Factor (Something you have): Smartphone authenticator app (TOTP), hardware security key (FIDO2 / YubiKey), smartcard. 3. Inherence Factor (Something you are): Biometrics (fingerprint, retina scan, facial recognition). MFA requires presenting credentials from at least two DIFFERENT categories.", follow_up: "Is entering a password and answering a security question true 2FA? Answer: No, both are knowledge factors (1FA).")

#interview-compact(num: 98, q: "Explain the core architectural tenets of Zero Trust Security.", a: "Tenet 1: 'Never Trust, Always Verify'—treat all network traffic as hostile, whether inside the perimeter or on the Internet. Tenet 2: Assume Breach—minimize blast radius through microsegmentation. Tenet 3: Least Privilege Access—grant access strictly on a just-in-time, just-enough basis. Tenet 4: Continuous Posture Verification—verify endpoint health, user identity, and behavioral anomalies continuously.", follow_up: "What Cisco platform orchestrates Zero Trust Network Access? Answer: Cisco Duo Security and Cisco Identity Services Engine (ISE).")

#interview-compact(num: 99, q: "Detail 4 common Social Engineering attack vectors targeting enterprise networks.", a: "1. Phishing: Deceptive mass emails containing malicious links or attachments. 2. Spear Phishing: Highly customized attacks targeting specific individuals using reconnaissance data. 3. Whaling: Targeted phishing directed at high-profile executives (CEOs, CFOs) for wire fraud. 4. Vishing / Smishing: Voice call / SMS deception impersonating IT support to obtain OTP tokens or passwords.", follow_up: "What technical defense prevents spoofed domain emails? Answer: SPF, DKIM, and DMARC DNS records.")

#interview-compact(num: 100, q: "Explain Cisco Configuration Register values 0x2102 and 0x2142 in disaster recovery.", a: "0x2102: Default production register. Directs router to load IOS from Flash, read startup-config from NVRAM, and set console baud rate to 9600 bps. 0x2142: Password recovery register. Bit 6 (0x0040) is set to 1, instructing bootstrap loader to bypass NVRAM startup-config, booting into a fresh setup dialog without deleting configurations.", follow_up: "Command to return register back to production after recovery? Answer: 'config-register 0x2102' followed by 'write memory'.")


