= DOMAIN 6: AUTOMATION, PROGRAMMABILITY & CLOUD (Q101 – Q120)

#interview-compact(num: 101, q: "Explain the difference between the Control Plane and Data Plane in SDN.", a: "Data Plane (Forwarding): Physical hardware ASICs that forward packets based on forwarding tables (FIB). Control Plane: Software logic (OSPF, BGP, STP) that builds the forwarding tables. Software-Defined Networking (SDN) decouples the control plane from distributed hardware and centralizes it into a software controller.", follow_up: "What is the Management Plane? Answer: Administrative access interfaces (CLI, GUI, APIs).")

#interview-compact(num: 102, q: "What is the difference between RESTCONF and NETCONF?", a: "Both are programmatic network APIs based on the YANG data modeling language. NETCONF (RFC 6241) uses SSH transport and XML formatting. RESTCONF (RFC 8040) is an HTTP-based RESTful implementation using standard HTTP verbs (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and JSON or XML payloads.", follow_up: "What data modeling language do they share? Answer: YANG (RFC 6020).")

#interview-compact(num: 103, q: "What are the common HTTP verbs used in RESTful network APIs?", a: "GET: Retrieve data (equivalent to 'show' commands). POST: Create a new resource (e.g. create a new VLAN or interface). PUT: Replace/update an existing resource entirely. PATCH: Partially update an existing resource. DELETE: Remove a resource (equivalent to 'no' commands).", follow_up: "What HTTP status code indicates success with data? Answer: 200 OK (201 Created for POST).")

#interview-compact(num: 104, q: "What is the difference between JSON and YAML data formats?", a: "JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) uses curly braces '{}' and square brackets '[]' with key-value pairs; strictly machine-readable. YAML (YAML Ain't Markup Language) uses clean whitespace indentation without braces; highly human-readable, widely used in Ansible playbooks and Kubernetes manifests.", follow_up: "Which is stricter about indentation? Answer: YAML.")

#interview-compact(num: 105, q: "Explain the difference between Ansible, Puppet, and Chef in configuration management.", a: "Ansible is agentless (uses SSH/Python) and operates via push-based playbooks written in YAML. Puppet is agent-based, pull-based, and uses a declarative domain-specific language (DSL). Chef is agent-based, pull-based, and uses procedural Ruby recipes.", follow_up: "Which is most popular in network engineering? Answer: Ansible (because network switches lack agent install access).")

#interview-compact(num: 106, q: "What is Cisco DNA Center and what is its role in SDA (Software-Defined Access)?", a: "Cisco DNA Center is an enterprise network management controller providing automation, analytics, and policy. In SD-Access, it builds a campus overlay network using LISP (Locator/ID Separation Protocol) for control plane, VXLAN for data plane, and TrustSec for group-based security policies.", follow_up: "What protocol encapsulates packets in SDA? Answer: VXLAN.")

#interview-compact(num: 107, q: "What is an Overlay network versus an Underlay network?", a: "The Underlay is the physical Layer 3 routed network infrastructure (cables, spine-leaf switches, routers running OSPF/BGP). The Overlay is a logical virtualized network built on top of the underlay using encapsulation tunnels (e.g., VXLAN, GRE, IPsec) to transport customer traffic independent of physical topology.", follow_up: "Does the underlay know about customer VLANs? Answer: No, underlay only routes tunnel packets.")

#interview-compact(num: 108, q: "What is YANG (RFC 6020) and how does it model network device state?", a: "YANG (Yet Another Next Generation) is a data modeling language used to define configuration and state data for network devices accessed via NETCONF and RESTCONF. It defines data schemas: container, list, leaf, and leaf-list elements. Distinguishes between 'config true' (read-write settings like interface IP) and 'config false' (read-only state data like packet counters).", follow_up: "What is OpenConfig? Answer: Vendor-neutral YANG models developed by Google and network operators to eliminate proprietary CLI formats.")

#interview-compact(num: 109, q: "Explain the Cisco SD-WAN 4-plane architecture and component roles.", a: "1. Orchestration Plane (vBond): Authenticates and white-lists routers, orchestrates initial control connections through NAT. 2. Management Plane (vManage): Centralized Web GUI for configuration templates, monitoring, and software upgrades. 3. Control Plane (vSmart): Facilitates OMP (Overlay Management Protocol) route distribution, encryption keys, and traffic policies. 4. Data Plane (vEdge / cEdge): Physical/virtual routers that forward user traffic over encrypted IPsec tunnels.", follow_up: "What protocol runs exclusively between vSmart and edge routers? Answer: OMP (Overlay Management Protocol).")

#interview-compact(num: 110, q: "Explain the role of Python Netmiko and REST APIs in modern network automation.", a: "Netmiko is a multi-vendor Python library built on top of Paramiko that simplifies SSH connections to network devices, automatically handling CLI paging, prompt detection, and configuration changes. REST APIs (using Python 'requests' library) provide structured, programmatic JSON interaction with modern controllers (DNA Center, Meraki, vManage) without screen scraping.", follow_up: "Why is REST API preferred over SSH scraping in production? Answer: REST APIs return structured JSON objects, eliminating regex parsing errors when CLI output formats change.")

#interview-compact(num: 111, q: "Explain Git version control workflows in Network-as-Code (NaC).", a: "Network configurations are treated as software code stored in Git repositories. Workflows: 1. Engineer creates feature branch ('git checkout -b add-vlan'). 2. Updates YAML variable files. 3. Commits changes with descriptive message ('git commit'). 4. Pushes to remote and opens Pull Request (PR). 5. CI/CD pipeline (GitHub Actions/GitLab CI) executes automated syntax validation and pre-deployment tests before merging to main.", follow_up: "What is the rollback procedure if a Git deployment causes an outage? Answer: 'git revert [commit-id]', which automatically triggers CI/CD pipeline to deploy previous working config.")

#interview-compact(num: 112, q: "Compare Cloud Service Models: IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS under Shared Responsibility.", a: "IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service, AWS EC2): Cloud provider manages physical hardware, cooling, and hypervisor; customer manages OS, patching, networking, and applications. PaaS (Platform as a Service, AWS Elastic Beanstalk): Provider manages hardware, OS, and runtime; customer manages application code and data. SaaS (Software as a Service, M365, Salesforce): Provider manages entire stack; customer only configures user access.", follow_up: "In IaaS, who is responsible for OS security patches? Answer: The customer.")

#interview-compact(num: 113, q: "Compare Cloud Deployment Models: Public, Private, Hybrid, and Multi-Cloud.", a: "Public Cloud (AWS, Azure): Multi-tenant infrastructure owned by cloud provider, accessible over Internet. Private Cloud (OpenStack, VMware Cloud Foundation): Dedicated single-tenant infrastructure owned and operated exclusively by one organization. Hybrid Cloud: Integrates on-premises private datacenter with public cloud via DirectConnect or IPsec VPN. Multi-Cloud: Utilizes services from multiple distinct public cloud providers to avoid vendor lock-in.", follow_up: "What AWS service provides private, dedicated physical fiber connectivity from datacenter to AWS? Answer: AWS Direct Connect (DX).")

#interview-compact(num: 114, q: "Detail AWS VPC networking components: CIDR, Subnets, IGW, and NAT Gateways.", a: "VPC: Private virtual network defined by IPv4 CIDR (e.g. 10.0.0.0/16). Public Subnet: Has route table entry pointing '0.0.0.0/0' to an Internet Gateway (IGW); instances receive public IPs. Private Subnet: Route table points '0.0.0.0/0' to a NAT Gateway located in a public subnet, enabling outbound internet access (for software updates) while blocking unsolicited inbound connections.", follow_up: "Compare Security Groups vs Network ACLs (NACLs). Answer: Security Groups are stateful and operate at instance level; NACLs are stateless and operate at subnet boundary.")

#interview-compact(num: 115, q: "Compare Virtual Machines (VMs) vs Containers (Docker) in modern cloud architecture.", a: "Virtual Machines: Virtualize physical hardware using a Type 1 (bare-metal ESXi/KVM) or Type 2 hypervisor; each VM packages a full guest operating system, kernel, and binaries (heavyweight, gigabytes in size, minutes to boot). Containers: Virtualize the operating system kernel; multiple isolated user-space containers share the host OS kernel (lightweight, megabytes in size, milliseconds to start).", follow_up: "What container orchestration platform manages automated scaling and self-healing? Answer: Kubernetes (K8s).")

#interview-compact(num: 116, q: "Compare Cisco Autonomous APs vs Lightweight APs (LAP) with Wireless LAN Controller (WLC).", a: "Autonomous APs ('Thick APs'): Standalone devices where RF management, SSID config, security policies, and bridging are configured individually on each AP. Lightweight APs ('Thin APs'): Lack independent configuration; automatically discover and bind to a centralized WLC using CAPWAP (Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points) tunnels (UDP 5246 control, UDP 5247 data).", follow_up: "What discovery mechanisms does a LAP use to find its WLC? Answer: 1. DHCP Option 43, 2. DNS query 'CISCO-CAPWAP-CONTROLLER.local', 3. Broadcast subnet flood.")

#interview-compact(num: 117, q: "Explain the Split-MAC architecture in Cisco centralized wireless networks.", a: "Split-MAC divides 802.11 processing between LAP and WLC. Real-time MAC functions handled locally by LAP: Frame transmission/acknowledgment, beacon generation, probe response, real-time power measurement. Management MAC functions handled centrally by WLC: Association/re-association requests, 802.1X authentication, Roaming coordination, and RF resource management (RRM).", follow_up: "What is Cisco FlexConnect mode? Answer: Allows LAP at remote branch to switch client data traffic locally if WAN link to centralized WLC goes down.")

#interview-compact(num: 118, q: "Compare Wireless RF Bands: 2.4 GHz vs 5 GHz vs 6 GHz (Wi-Fi 6E / Wi-Fi 7).", a: "2.4 GHz: Better wall penetration, longer range, but only 3 non-overlapping 20 MHz channels (Channels 1, 6, 11) and heavy interference (Bluetooth, microwaves). 5 GHz: Shorter range, higher absorption, but provides 24+ non-overlapping channels (20/40/80/160 MHz) delivering gigabit throughput. 6 GHz (Wi-Fi 6E/7): Clean spectrum from 5.925 to 7.125 GHz, zero legacy device interference, supports up to 320 MHz channels.", follow_up: "Why should adjacent APs never use the same 2.4 GHz channel? Answer: Prevents Co-Channel Interference (CCI), which forces APs to share airtime.")

#interview-compact(num: 119, q: "Detail API Authentication Mechanisms: Basic Auth vs Bearer Token vs OAuth 2.0.", a: "Basic Auth: Transmits base64-encoded 'username:password' in HTTP Authorization header with every request (vulnerable if TLS intercepted). Bearer Token: Client exchanges credentials once for a cryptographically signed token (e.g. JWT); client presents 'Authorization: Bearer <token>' on subsequent API requests; token expires automatically. OAuth 2.0: Delegated authorization framework where a resource owner authorizes third-party access without sharing passwords.", follow_up: "What is an API Rate Limit? Answer: Policy restricting number of API requests per minute (e.g. 100 req/min) returning HTTP 429 Too Many Requests if exceeded.")

#interview-compact(num: 120, q: "Compare Streaming Model-Driven Telemetry vs Traditional SNMP Polling.", a: "Traditional SNMP: Pull-based; centralized NMS queries network devices periodically (e.g. every 5 minutes); introduces CPU polling overhead and misses transient micro-bursts and sub-second link flaps. Streaming Telemetry: Push-based; network devices continuously stream real-time operational data subscriptions via gRPC/TCP and Protobuf encoding at sub-second intervals, enabling instant AI-driven anomaly detection.", follow_up: "What protocol provides high-speed binary serialization in streaming telemetry? Answer: Google Protocol Buffers (Protobuf).")

